2186045417] [17592186491072 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "afa62c7a-3eae-53d1-8bfb-2c44ea119440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491073 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491073 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491073 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491073 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491073 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b0cf17cb-025b-52d2-912c-740839b549f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491088 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491088 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491088 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491088 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491088 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d643483d-5fbe-5334-8c77-295c62b8921e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491114 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253558" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491114 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491114 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $B=(\\pi ,4).$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $inf(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491114 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ca69e5c-83dc-519f-8c14-c4c499eb6220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491114 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le \\pi $ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,\\pi ]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>\\pi $ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\pi ]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(B)=max(-\\infty ,\\pi ]=\\pi .\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\pi \\notin B.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B.$\n\nThe set of lower bounds of $B$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\le \\pi \\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $inf(B)=\\pi \\notin B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491137 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{(-1)^{n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n+1}}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "402a4d55-ba8d-55eb-b6d0-1bc9813966c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491137 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=(-1)^{n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n+1}},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/69/690c0466ffac72597e9378539d1540d6baaa49c7cbe77bee8cc83ab00f065635.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating sequence converging to $0.$ The maximum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmax(A)={a}_{2}=(-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2+1}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\ge {a}_{2}$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x<{a}_{2}$ cannot be an upper bound because ${a}_{2},$ an element of $A,$ is greater than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}},\\infty )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is the maximum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\in A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491140 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491140 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491140 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491140 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $B,$ defined as\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+1}:n\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $B?$\n\n$inf(B)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "423e9057-895c-5e42-b0de-0a5a3aa83973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491140 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of a set $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the infimum of $B$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=0,1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}+1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{(1)^{2}+1} & =\\frac{1}{